6Dfhj0kddctwPa2xlXFyWbhLtUOOEX_Vk
AI
firm at north of $1.5tn.

The numbers are jaw-dropping and speak to the speed of the AI boom. Anthrop=
ic was founded in 2021. That=E2=80=99s only five years ago, yet already it =
has grown to a valuation bigger than 98% of the top companies on the US sto=
ck market.

Anthropic detailed rapidly rising revenue in the prospectus: $41m in 2023, =
$386m in 2024, $4.585bn in 2025 (a yearly increase of more than 1,000%), th=
en $16.286bn in the first six months of 2026 alone. The company disclosed t=
hat, as of the end of June, it had raised $141.1bn.

Despite the increasing revenue, Anthropic disclosed a net loss of $80bn for=
 the first half of 2026, mostly due to a one-time $72.4bn charge. It lost $=
36bn the year prior.

Anthropic=E2=80=99s initial public offering will rock the US stock market a=
nd probably global markets as well. The infusion of at least a trillion dol=
lars in value is likely to influence investor appetites for every kind of t=
echnology stock. That a company of that magnitude can be spun up in five ye=
ars speaks to the breakneck pace of AI development and financing. Despite c=
alls to slow down from Anthropic=E2=80=99s CEO, Dario Amodei, the artificia=
l intelligence industry is still defined by a careening intensity that=E2=
=80=99s taking the world=E2=80=99s finances along for the ride.

Such rapid growth demonstrates an upside for company founders for now. They=
 can build a business that creates life-changing, globe-spanning wealth in =
just half a decade.

For the rest of us, the speed of growth spells precarity. Anthropic, a comp=
any that could determine the direction of the entire US economy, is going p=
ublic before a baby born in its founding year has started kindergarten.

