Meta pays out


The Meta logo. Photograph: Beno=C3=AEt Tessier/Reuters


Settlements over the safety of social networks are growing so large that Me=
ta is reporting significant drags on its business.

In August, the biggest social media company in the world agreed to a $17bn =
settlement with dozens of state attorneys general to put claims it had enda=
ngered children to rest without admitting guilt.

In a quarterly earnings report last week, issued the same afternoon as a ju=
dge ordered the company to pay the state of New Mexico $25bn, Meta reported=
 net income significantly below Wall Street analysts=E2=80=99 expectations =
because of a payment towards the settlement with attorneys general, this on=
e $9.9bn.

The dent in Meta=E2=80=99s business is a sign that child safety activists a=
re succeeding. In the Los Angeles social media addiction lawsuit that took =
place earlier this year, lawyers for the plaintiff made it clear they=E2=80=
=99re intent on making it untenable for Meta to operate the current version=
 of Instagram. They want to render endangering children, which they believe=
 Meta is doing, too expensive to continue. They hope to push the company to=
 the point where business as usual is untenable because of the financial ri=
sks of safety.

Activists can=E2=80=99t celebrate yet, though. Despite the hefty payments, =
Meta still reported a 26% increase in quarterly revenue up to $64.6bn.

