;">Lula accuses Meta of suppression, then wins with WhatsApp</h2></div></td=
></tr>
<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Less than two weeks ago, Brazilian preside=
nt Luiz In=C3=A1cio Lula da Silva was accusing Meta of suppressing his post=
s on Instagram and Facebook and attempting to derail his campaign. Fl=C3=A1=
vio Bolsonaro, his opponent and the son of the previous president, had all =
the momentum after the country=E2=80=99s first round of elections. Lula=E2=
=80=99s supporters felt like they would be crushed under the rightwing wave=
 sweeping over Latin America. Meta denied bias but acknowledged technical f=
ailures in response to Lula=E2=80=99s accusations.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">We will likely never know the full truth o=
f what Meta did or did not do for Lula. Individual algorithmic moderation m=
istakes =E2=80=93 usually a few egregious ones among millions of otherwise =
unobjectionable choices =E2=80=93 seem like bias in times of heightened pol=
itical tension. That=E2=80=99s what Meta says happened in Brazil. Raging ag=
ainst those errors makes for engaging posts, which outsizes the glitches=E2=
=80=99 significance. The history of accusations against Meta of political i=
nterference is arcane and complicated. The motives of everyone involved are=
 suspect. Independent studies conducted after the 2020 US election showed t=
hat <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEm=
Fuifc_XG18IR7l0i3omFT-LEnbcI1ckSgVec6y0BNm-LOESkhfw0KB5ZBkFouk6fDWxpqDfJ04l=
QTK8k6S7XQ6nXUK90eAShFwwwoi0y7y6FQg9SZIGeIFWGfyM5julD2zamOZGMFPunjwAA6DSRlt=
d9KBjp7OfhpqnxsKB-FsIHoCijc2iN8DWO9qeLjexC6sljs70zn6oF9G-G3vhWL9MkcQwtyf9XC=
eLi0l2fpQnFQfN6_zY2ARmeSyFDep4zDWEYjXr4jy-pH208tQd0E4WQa_UqFu5YWM/4vf/SJ3Jy=
LPxRPixYOWs4-ltyA/h37/h001.sDlvd7WSpbKR_nuAmeZ62hQQrsnfK8_7LUzV65qUmaY" sty=
le=3D"color: #0077B6;">rightwing content traveled further on social media</=
a> than leftwing posts do. That small data point indicates it may not have =
been the heavy thumb of Meta on the political scales in Brazil.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">During the second-round runoff, Lula, 80, =
bought a smartphone for the first time and assembled WhatsApp groups of mil=
lions of supporters who mobilized in response to the threat of yet another =
Bolsonaro. (WhatsApp is owned by Meta.) At the helm of these channels was A=
ndr=C3=A9 Janones, the captain of =E2=80=9CLula=E2=80=99s army=E2=80=9D and=
 a federal deputy from the state of Minas Gerais. Janones issued daily soci=
al media challenges to the supporters he called his =E2=80=9Csoldiers=E2=80=
=9D.</p>
</div></td></tr>
<tr><td align=3D"center" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size:0px;padding:=
